{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS enhances cluster governance with new IAM condition keys - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced seven new IAM condition keys for managing cluster creation and configuration APIs. These keys enhance governance controls through IAM policies and Service Control Policies (SCPs), allowing organizations to enforce security and compliance requirements consistently across all clusters. The new keys include:\n\n1. eks:endpointPublicAccess: Enforces private-only API endpoints.\n2. eks:endpointPrivateAccess: Requires private API endpoints.\n3. eks:encryptionConfigProviderKeyArns: Mandates the use of customer-managed AWS KMS keys for secrets encryption.\n4. eks:kubernetesVersion: Restricts clusters to approved Kubernetes versions.\n5. eks:deletionProtection: Mandates deletion protection for production workloads.\n6. eks:controlPlaneScalingTier: Specifies control plane scaling tiers.\n7. eks:zonalShiftEnabled: Enables zonal shift capabilities for high availability.\n\nThese condition keys apply to CreateCluster, UpdateClusterConfig, UpdateClusterVersion, and AssociateEncryptionConfig APIs, and integrate seamlessly with AWS Organizations SCPs for centralized governance across accounts. The new IAM condition keys are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS is available at no additional charge. For more information, refer to the Amazon EKS User Guide and the Service Authorization Reference for Amazon EKS, as well as the AWS Organizations documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/amazon-eks-iam-condition-keys/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS now supports customer-routed control plane egress - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) has introduced a new capability called customer-routed control plane egress. This feature allows organizations to route outbound traffic from the Kubernetes API server through their own Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). The traffic includes admission webhook callbacks, OpenID Connect (OIDC) provider lookups, and aggregate API server requests. By routing this traffic through their VPC, organizations can control the routing, security groups, and egress path. This is beneficial for organizations with data security requirements, compliance mandates, or private network infrastructure. To enable this feature, set controlPlaneEggressMode to CUSTOMER\\_ROUTED when creating or updating a cluster. To enforce this configuration organization-wide, use the eks:controlPlaneEgressMode IAM condition key with AWS Organizations Service Control Policies. Customer-routed control plane egress is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS is available. For more information, refer to the Amazon EKS User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/amazon-eks-customer-routed-control-plane-egress/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon EKS introduces enhanced network security policies - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced enhanced network security policies to improve the security posture for Kubernetes workloads and integrations with external destinations. These enhancements include the ability to centrally enforce network access filters across the entire cluster and leverage Domain Name System (DNS) based policies for securing egress traffic. EKS now supports Kubernetes NetworkPolicies in the Amazon VPC Container Network Interface (VPC CNI) plugin, enabling pod-to-pod communication segmentation at a namespace level. Cluster admins can now prevent unauthorized access to cluster-external resources using egress rules that filter traffic based on Fully Qualified Domain Names (FQDN). These new features are available in all commercial AWS Regions for new EKS clusters running Kubernetes version 1.29 or later, with support for existing clusters to follow. ClusterNetworkPolicy is available in all EKS cluster launch modes using VPC CNI v1.21.1 or later, while DNS-based policies are only supported in EKS Auto Mode-launched EC2 instances","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/amazon-eks-enhanced-network-security-policies/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"configuring-vpc-endpoints-for-private-aws-service-access","skill_description":"Configures VPC endpoints (interface and gateway) for private AWS service access using AWS PrivateLink. Use when setting up secure private connectivity to S3, DynamoDB, and other AWS services without internet gateway, NAT device, or public IP addresses. Covers endpoint creation, security groups, route tables, and DNS configuration.\n\nServices: ec2, vpc, s3, dynamodb\nTasks: configure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: networking","skill_name":"configuring-vpc-endpoints-for-private-aws-service-access"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"waf","skill_description":"Configures AWS WAF to filter web traffic: creating web access control lists (web ACLs) on CloudFront, Application Load Balancers, API Gateway, and AppSync; AWS Managed Rules tuned in Count mode; rate-based rules for HTTP floods; IP set and geographic match rules; Bot Control (Common and Targeted); turning bot labels into a confidence signal; stripping spoofed inbound x-amzn-waf-* headers; recovering the real client IP behind a CDN; Fraud Control (account takeover and account creation fraud prevention); and logging and request sampling. Use when the user wants to protect a web application or API from common exploits, bots, credential stuffing, fake-account creation, or HTTP floods at the application layer (layer 7). Routes to the right per-task procedure in references. Do NOT use for L3/L4 DDoS protection (shieldadvanced skill), multi-account WAF rollout (firewallmanager skill), CloudFront configuration (cloudfront skill), or Route 53 health checks or records (route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: waf, wafv2, cloudfront, elb, apigateway, appsync\nTasks: configure, secure, debug\nPersona: developer, devops, security-engineer\nWorkload: security, web-application","skill_name":"waf"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check AWS docs for CIS EKS Benchmark v1.8.0 controls 5.4.1 and 5.4.2"}
